    <div class="container">
        <h1>Construction of Polynomial \( p(x) \)</h1>

        <p>To satisfy the condition that the curve \( X := \{p(x) = p(y)\} \) has at least 3 irreducible components (where not all are linear) for a prime degree \( n=19 \), we must use <strong>Dickson Polynomials of the first kind</strong>.</p>

        <h2>1. Selecting the Polynomial Form</h2>
        <p>For a prime degree \( n \), the factorization of \( p(x) - p(y) \) into multiple components occurs if \( p(x) \) is equivalent to a Dickson polynomial \( D_n(x, a) \). The general form of the Dickson polynomial is:</p>
        <div class="math-block">
            \[ D_n(x, a) = \sum_{k=0}^{\lfloor n/2 \rfloor} \frac{n}{n-k} \binom{n-k}{k} (-a)^k x^{n-2k} \]
        </div>

        <h2>2. Satisfying the Constraints</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Monic and Odd:</strong> By definition, \( D_{19}(x, a) \) is monic (the leading coefficient is 1) and contains only odd powers of \( x \), making it an odd function.</li>
            <li><strong>Linear Coefficient:</strong> The linear term occurs at \( 19 - 2k = 1 \), which implies \( k = 9 \). The coefficient is:
                \[ \text{Coeff}(x^1) = \frac{19}{19-9} \binom{19-9}{9} (-a)^9 = \frac{19}{10} \cdot 10 \cdot (-a^9) = -19 a^9 \]
            </li>
            <li><strong>Solving for \( a \):</strong> Given the linear coefficient is \( -19 \):
                \[ -19 a^9 = -19 \implies a^9 = 1 \]
                To ensure real coefficients as requested, we choose the real root <span class="highlight">\( a = 1 \)</span>.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>3. Final Polynomial Definition</h2>
        <p>The polynomial is \( p(x) = D_{19}(x, 1) \). This can be expressed using the generating property:</p>
        <div class="math-block">
            \[ p\left(w + \frac{1}{w}\right) = w^{19} + \frac{1}{w^{19}} \]
        </div>

        <h2>4. Calculation of \( p(19) \)</h2>
        <p>To find \( p(19) \), we set \( x = w + \frac{1}{w} = 19 \). We solve for \( w \):</p>
        <div class="math-block">
            \[ w^2 - 19w + 1 = 0 \implies w = \frac{19 + \sqrt{357}}{2} \]
        </div>
        <p>Substituting this into the Dickson property:</p>
        <div class="math-block">
            \[ p(19) = \left( \frac{19 + \sqrt{357}}{2} \right)^{19} + \left( \frac{19 - \sqrt{357}}{2} \right)^{19} \]
        </div>
        <p>This value is the 19th term of the Lucas sequence \( L_n \) defined by \( L_n = 19L_{n-1} - L_{n-2} \) with \( L_0 = 2, L_1 = 19 \).</p>

        <div class="math-block" style="text-align: center;">
            <span class="highlight">Result:</span><br>
            \( p(19) = \left( \frac{19 + \sqrt{357}}{2} \right)^{19} + \left( \frac{19 - \sqrt{357}}{2} \right)^{19} \)
        </div>
